// BUGGY taint-egress policy — "never contact an outsider after touching
// confidential data".  The `when temporal` clause is a *bare temporal trigger*
// predicate (not an aggregate, not compared to a cap): the confidential-data
// read (`ReadConfidential`) taints the agent's context, and the policy's
// scope (`Send`) names the egress that is forbidden while the taint is held.
//
// The one-word bug vs. the correct variant: `response` instead of `request`.
//   * `response`-trigger (BUGGY):  the read's *response* alone taints the
//     context, leaving a window in which the egress is admitted while the
//     agent still holds private data (the exfiltration hole).  VIOLATED.
//   * `request`-trigger  (CORRECT): the read's *request* already taints the
//     context, so the egress is admitted only when the agent is NOT holding
//     private data (the NoExfiltration rule holds).  PROVEN.
//
// Schema: Drupe namespace.  `ReadConfidential` carries `output.label`; the
// `private` label marks the data as confidential.
// The response event carries the same `output` as its request, so
// `response{ output.label: "private" }` is well-formed.
//
// Counterexample (this variant):
//   ReadConfidential  -> agent now holds private data (taint set)
//   Send              -> admitted (unguarded) -> EXFILTRATION  <-- violation
//
// See: rot/agent_harness.py (the exfiltration model this shape lowers to),
//      rot/shapes.py  (build_taint_egress_ir -- the hand-written oracle).

@id("taint_egress_buggy")
forbid (
    principal,
    action == Drupe::Action::"Send",
    resource
)
when temporal {
    formerly within 24h Drupe::Action::"ReadConfidential"::response{ output.label: "private" }
};
